ency quorum  rights with cassandra  as uber's products and services evolved  we started seeing complex storage  interactions that required multi-row and  multi-table rights  so we had to build an application layer  framework to orchestrate this operation  by leveraging the saga pattern  but the underlying storage platform with  cassandra being eventually consistent it  often led to inconsistencies that had to  be manually corrected  what it translated in the real world  would be a rider seeing two different  drivers dispatch to them so imagine in  you being out there near your office and  there are two drivers who arrive to pick  you up  it's it's it's bit confusing for anyone  out there as we scaled globally to  millions of active users  even a notion of consistency became  harder to achieve at our application  level in addition to all of this as we  scaled we started seeing uh horizontal  scaling bottlenecks as well in the  overall architecture primarily because  of the way we started with our  application layer with ringpop  this is when we started tracing our  steps back to the properties of a  traditional sql based storage engine  that provides asset guarantees  we started defining a transaction  storage engine that would provide  consistency as the primary capability  one of the things that has been in  mainstream technology was new sequel  storage engines that combined the  scalability of a nosql architecture with  strong asset guarantees this is when we  came across google cloud spanner now so  that definitely sounds like you know a  lot of hit trials and and then figuring  out what's working and what's not  working definitely the eventual  consistency what not was not the aspect  that could be lived with especially with  this scale so what were the thought  process and methodologies that you were  exploring when transitioning from nosql  to new sql when we were thinking about  the next chapter for uber's fulfillment  platform the focus on consistency became