ments were  sounds like they were so critical to um  this whole setup now pratham how do you  optimize for cost when using spanner at  that scale  yeah we did a few things to optimize for  cost for uber so for example we  optimized node utilization so in case of  a multi-region configuration a read can  be issued to the leader or to the  replica if a strong read is issued to  the replica it incurs the extra cost of  latency until the replica has the latest  snapshot to serve the strong read  to reduce the impact on latency  we leveraged google's private service  connect and we routed the strong reads  to the leader region and all the stale  reads were routed to the replica only  region  this helped us in maximizing the  utilization of the nodes across the  leader and the replica region  there are other cool things that ankit  and team have done  on the latency and cost optimization  yeah in addition to the private service  connect  which optimizes how the traffic routing  works across the leader and replica  region we we also did an auto scaler and  on-prem cache to improve the overall  latency and cost  as you're aware that uber's traffic  pattern is variable by the time of day  and week being provisioned for maximum  capacity at all time based resources  to take advantage of cloud spanner's  elasticity we built an auto scaler that  constantly tunes the number of nodes  based on a cpu utilization target so as  the traffic is variable based on a  constant cpu utilization target you're  able to achieve maximum elasticity out  of the cloud spanner cluster  similarly uh our most of our workload is  very need heavy so we built an on-prem  cache to improve the latency and cost  to maintain the same consistency as  spanner we only route this stale reads  to spanner and we allow our cache to  serve  snapshot isolation based on spanish true  time we built a query caching  functionality to serve just the sale  reads from cache that hydra that is  hydrated by strong reads and